The component pattern is actually pretty standard — we're just rendering the items from the store and dispatching actions to modify them. The real value-add of Redux Toolkit is in the consistency of the reducer patterns and the tooling around action deserialization.

Here's the full CartComponent implementation that leverages the slice defined earlier:

```jsx
const CartComponent = () => {
  const dispatch = useDispatch();
  const { items } = useSelector(state => state.cart);

  const updateQuantity = (id, newQuantity) => {
    if (newQuantity === 0) {
      dispatch(removeItem(id));
    } else {
      dispatch(updateItem({ id, quantity: newQuantity }));
    }
  };

  return (
    <div className="shopping-cart">
      <h2>Your Cart</h2>
      {items.length === 0 ? (
        <p className="empty-cart">Your cart is empty.</p>
      ) : (
        <ul className="cart-items">
          {items.map(item => (
            <li key={item.id} className="cart-item">
              <div className="item-info">
                <img src={item.image} alt={item.name} className="item-image" />
                <div className="item-details">
                  <h3>{item.name}</h3>
                  <p>${item.price}</p>
                  <div className="quantity-controls">
                    <button onClick={() => updateQuantity(item.id, item.quantity - 1)}>-</button>
                    <span>{item.quantity}</span>
                    <button onClick={() => updateQuantity(item.id, item.quantity + 1)}>+</button>
                  </div>
                </div>
              </div>
              <button onClick={() => dispatch(removeItem(item.id))} className="remove-btn">Remove</button>
            </li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      )}
      <div className="cart-total">
        <strong>Total: ${items.reduce((sum, item) => sum + (item.price * item.quantity), 0)}</strong>
      </div>
      <button className="checkout-btn" disabled={items.length === 0}>
        Proceed to Checkout
      </button>
    </div>
  );
};
```

I'd add an `updateItem` action to the cart slice for this functionality — either as part of the same reducer or separately. The checkout button should dispatch a `checkout` action that captures the cart state before clearing the items.

